# --- secrets ---
.env

# --- config\: your settings, not your secrets -----------------------------
# Real config holds addresses, hostnames and paths - identifying, though not
# secret, so it follows the .env rule rather than the media\ one: it travels in
# your personal backup zip, it never reaches git or a public release. The
# *.example.ini templates and the README DO ship, so a fresh install has
# something to copy. fleet.json is tracked on purpose: model/effort per desk is
# fleet.json USED to be tracked as product. It is not: the desks map is keyed
# by session id, so it carries this instance's project names - found shipping
# a real one on 2026-08-14, in a repo whose only job is installing a stranger
# a fresh Omnius. It now follows the same rule as every other real config
# file: the .example ships, the real one is yours and travels in the backup.
config/*
!config/README.md
!config/*.example.ini
!config/fleet.example.json

# --- machine-local runtime (regenerates itself) ---
state/
**/.claude/settings.local.json
# The zip's birth certificate: pack.ps1 stamps it, install.ps1's attach step
# reads it, and it must never be committed - it describes an INSTALL, not the
# product.
RELEASE-COMMIT

# --- projects are their own git repos — the root repo never tracks them ---
projects/*
!projects/.gitkeep

# --- daybook notes: personal, and no longer in git (2026-08-14) ------------
# Tracked from 2026-07-31 for per-edit history, back when the repo doubled as
# a backup. It does not any more - his words: "repo is no longer for backups,
# it is now for fresh clean installs". Backups are the PC's job and Omnius's
# own (pack.ps1 -> the configured folder), and they DO include these.
# The app itself still ships; only the notes it holds are his.
daybook/notes/

# --- memory\ is this instance's BIOGRAPHY, not the product (2026-08-13) -----
# install.ps1:251 already said it - "the building instance's memory is its
# biography and never travels" - and pack.ps1 -Fresh enforced it. Git did not,
# and on 2026-08-12 a second install attached to this same remote and pushed
# its memory over the first machine's: status.md became the other fleet, and
# USER.md was rewritten from the near-empty seed, losing months of learned
# working preferences. Two instances, one repo, last writer wins - silently.
#
# So memory now travels the way it was designed to: in the personal backup zip
# (pack.ps1 with no flags includes memory\), and a fresh install seeds itself
# from templates\fresh\memory\, which stays tracked below. Old memory is still
# recoverable from history: git show <commit>:memory/orchestrator/status.md
memory/
!templates/fresh/memory/
# TEMPLATE memory is PRODUCT, not biography - `memory/` above is a bare
# pattern, so it matches at EVERY depth and swallowed these too:
# - the demo fleet's brief (found 2026-08-14: the shipped demo's first
#   instruction pointed at a file nobody outside this machine had)
# - the project template's memory seed (found 2026-08-15, diffing the live
#   instance against this repo: every project stamped from a public install
#   was born without memory/MEMORY.md or sessions/_template.md, while its
#   CLAUDE.md told desks to "copy _template.md")
!templates/demo-project/memory/
!templates/project/memory/

# --- asset archive: travels in the zip, stays out of git (binary bloat) ---
media/

# --- junk ---
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
dist/
build/
*.log
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
scratch/

# --- this instance's own paperwork, not the product (2026-08-14) ------------
# The repo exists to install a fresh Omnius - "el repo es para hacer
# instalacion fresh". These are notes ABOUT this instance, and pack.ps1 -Fresh
# already refuses to ship them; git was the only place they still travelled.
# They stay on disk and in the backup zip.
START-HERE.md
*HANDOVER*.md
docs/HANDOFF-*.md
